Key-expression algebra. No session, no bus: the only family whose whole
answer is a verdict plus an exit code, and the only one whose behaviour (not
just its help) is pinned — see `key-algebra.trycmd`.

```console
$ zenctl key --help
Keyexpr algebra, no session (RFC 03 §4's footguns, diagnosed)

Usage: zenctl key <COMMAND>

Commands:
  includes    Does `a` include every key `b` can name? Exit 0 yes / 1 no / 2 invalid
  intersects  Can `a` and `b` name a common key? Exit 0 yes / 1 no / 2 invalid
  canon       Canonicalize an expression, or print its parse error verbatim
  help        Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

$ zenctl key includes --help
Does `a` include every key `b` can name? Exit 0 yes / 1 no / 2 invalid

Usage: zenctl key includes [OPTIONS] <A> <B>

Arguments:
  <A>
          

  <B>
          

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl key intersects --help
Can `a` and `b` name a common key? Exit 0 yes / 1 no / 2 invalid.

When a 'no' is the convention's doing — `**` never crosses an `@`-chunk (RFC 03 §4 D2), `*` never matches a verbatim service origin (D4) — the output says so, with the citation.

Usage: zenctl key intersects [OPTIONS] <A> <B>

Arguments:
  <A>
          

  <B>
          

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl key canon --help
Canonicalize an expression, or print its parse error verbatim

Usage: zenctl key canon [OPTIONS] <EXPR>

Arguments:
  <EXPR>
          

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```
